{#- Triage's caps and identity: claude-triage@.service.j2, claude-triage-poll.service.j2,
claude-triage-config.env.j2 and 50-claude-triage.rules.j2 import these as `pins`. A template's own
assignment outranks every inventory value, so an agent PR that edits only host_vars cannot raise a
run's budget, turns or wall clock, run the unit as another user, or arm the poller. The host
package renderer puts the inventory above a component's defaults, so a component default would pin
nothing. The `claude-triage` template prefix is on the lander's approval list (operator decision,
2026-10-15). Each value is a literal. hosts/tests/test_claude_triage.py holds them equal to
the component's defaults/main.yml.
-#}
{%- set agent_user = "claude" -%}
{%- set agent_home = "/var/lib/claude" -%}
{%- set state_dir = "/var/lib/claude/triage" -%}
{%- set model = "opus" -%}
{%- set budget_usd = 10 -%}
{%- set max_turns = 60 -%}
{%- set run_timeout_s = 1800 -%}
{%- set daily_cap = 6 -%}
{#- Arming the poller is an operator decision too. -#}
{%- set poll_mode = "shadow" -%}
{#- The alerts whose fall opens an episode, each mapped to the profile that runs it. A key is an
alert name, or `<alert>:<service>` for one service of it, and the service key wins. `none` opens
nothing, and so does an alert the table does not name. A routed alert's rule must not select on log
text a session controls (the I6 test in hosts/tests/test_claude_triage.py). Widening the
table raises how many runs a day the poller asks for, so it is pinned too. -#}
{%- set poll_routes = {
  "ExtraProbeFailed": "triage",
  "ExtraProbeFailedCritical": "triage",
  "IngressProbeFailed": "triage",
  "IngressProbeFailedCritical": "triage",
  "K8sDeploymentZeroAvailable": "triage",
  "K8sContainerCrashLooping": "triage",
  "ScrapeTargetDown": "triage",
  "ExtraProbeFailed:wg-easy": "none",
} -%}
